s5p-mfc: keep the first VP9 or HEVC frame that came with the stream header.

After the firmware parses the stream header (SEQ_DONE), the buffer it was
in is only checked for unused bytes in H.264; for every other codec it is
marked done. In VP9 the header is the key frame itself, and in HEVC the
parameter sets usually share a buffer with the first IDR frame, so that frame
was dropped: the firmware then failed the next one with error 34 (no key
frame), and every frame after came out on an empty picture. Check VP9 and
HEVC the same way (Samsung's own driver keeps the rest of the buffer for
HEVC). On the Galaxy M20's MFC v11.20, with mainline's v10 support: VP9 and
HEVC 720p and 1080p now decode identically to libavcodec.

--- a/drivers/media/platform/samsung/s5p-mfc/s5p_mfc.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/samsung/s5p-mfc/s5p_mfc.c
@@ -545,8 +545,13 @@
 		else
 			ctx->state = MFCINST_HEAD_PARSED;
 
+		/* The header may share its buffer with the first frame: always
+		 * so in VP9 (the key frame is the header) and often in HEVC.
+		 * Keep that buffer for decoding unless all of it was used. */
 		if ((ctx->codec_mode == S5P_MFC_CODEC_H264_DEC ||
-			ctx->codec_mode == S5P_MFC_CODEC_H264_MVC_DEC) &&
+			ctx->codec_mode == S5P_MFC_CODEC_H264_MVC_DEC ||
+			ctx->codec_mode == S5P_MFC_CODEC_HEVC_DEC ||
+			ctx->codec_mode == S5P_MFC_CODEC_VP9_DEC) &&
 				!list_empty(&ctx->src_queue)) {
 			struct s5p_mfc_buf *src_buf;
 
